From 26c190c910c28a99c56b06ea21dc3bdb4822efdc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CoreRouter <noreply@corerouter>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 14:10:41 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] ospfd: max-lsa, a limit on the LSAs of other routers

ospfd installs every LSA it is flooded, without a limit. A wrong
redistribution elsewhere in the domain (a full BGP table into OSPF)
takes every router of the area out of memory: each LSA costs about
1.5 KiB in ospfd and each route about 0.6 KiB in zebra. In a lab with
FRR 10.7.1, 192 721 type-5 LSAs from one neighbor reached the database
in 5.2 s (175 436 in the first second), and ospfd grew to 293 MiB and
zebra to 120 MiB. A check from outside, polling "show ip ospf json",
comes after the whole flood; the limit has to be in ospfd.

Add "max-lsa N [threshold P] [warning-only] [ignore-time S]
[ignore-count C] [reset-time S]" under "router ospf", with the
semantics other implementations use (Cisco max-lsa, Junos
database-protection): only the LSAs of other routers count; a warning
at P % of N; above N the instance goes down for ignore-time (its
interfaces, so every adjacency, and the learned LSAs go), then comes
back; after ignore-count such periods it stays down until "clear ip
ospf max-lsa"; reset-time up resets the count. Going down as a whole,
instead of refusing the LSAs over the limit, keeps the databases of the
area consistent (RFC 2328, section 13): without adjacencies no router
routes through this one, while a router that keeps its adjacencies with
a partial database computes paths its neighbors do not.

The check is O(areas), once per event loop pass after an LSA of another
router is added (an event, scheduled from ospf_lsa_install()). Measured
with the same flood and "max-lsa 50000": the instance went down at
50 002 LSAs, 885 ms after the flood started; ospfd peaked at 84 MiB and
zebra at 42 MiB.

The down state is its own (ospf->max_lsa.state), not the "shutdown"
configuration flag: the running configuration does not change, and
ospf_if_up() and the external route handling look at ospf_is_down(),
which covers both. "show ip ospf" shows the count of LSAs of other
routers always, and the limit, the state, the ignore periods and the
time left when a limit is set (JSON: lsaOtherRoutersCounter, maxLsa).

Signed-off-by: CoreRouter <noreply@corerouter>
